Q: Windows API Serial Read Issues A: <p>Your Arduino is configured for <code>Serial.begin(9600);</code></p>
<p>But your Windows code uses <code>dcbSerialParams.BaudRate = CBR_115200;</code></p>
<p>That mismatch produces corrupted ASCII, especially noticeable at higher send rates.</p>
<p>A serial port is just a byte stream, <code>ReadFile()</code> returns <em>whatever bytes happen to be available at that moment</em> — not a full Arduino <code>println()</code>, not a comma-delimited record, and not necessarily aligned to <code>'\n'</code>.</p>
<p>So at low delays Windows might read:</p>
<ol>
<li>half a line</li>
<li>multiple lines at once</li>
</ol>
<p>Correct way :</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>std::string buffer;

// ...
    buffer += readBuffer;

    size_t pos;
    while ((pos = buffer.find('\\n')) != std::string::npos) {
        std::string line = buffer.substr(0, pos);
        buffer.erase(0, pos + 1);
    }
// ...
</code></pre>
